{\b Chapter 1 Introduction}\par
\par
{\b 1.1 Background of the Study}\par
Micro-credentials have become an increasingly important topic in higher education because they are associated with flexible learning, skills development, and employability. Compared with traditional degree programs, micro-credentials are generally shorter, more targeted, and more modular. They are often used to certify specific knowledge or competencies and are frequently presented as a way to support upskilling and reskilling in rapidly changing environments. In higher education, this development reflects broader shifts toward lifelong learning, digital delivery, and stronger alignment between education and labor-market needs.\par
\par
Ahsan et al. (2023) describe micro-credentials as competency-based forms of learning recognition that may function as an additional, alternative, complementary, or component part of a formal qualification. Reed et al. (2024) similarly emphasize that micro-credentials represent assessed achievement at a modular level rather than simple participation in short courses. These definitions suggest that micro-credentials are not important merely because they are short. Their significance lies in the possibility that they may certify meaningful learning in a more flexible form than traditional credentials.\par
\par
The growing interest in micro-credentials is also linked to policy concerns. OECD (2021) argues that the quality and value of micro-credentials depend heavily on recognition, transparency, and trust. OECD (2023) further notes that micro-credentials are increasingly promoted as mechanisms for lifelong learning and employability because they may widen access to learning and support faster responses to changing skills demands. However, the same literature also makes clear that flexibility alone does not create value. If a credential is not understood, recognized, or trusted, its usefulness may remain limited.\par
\par
These issues are especially important in IT-related education. Fields such as information technology, computer science, software engineering, information systems, and digital business change rapidly as new tools, platforms, and methods continue to emerge. Students in these fields often need more agile ways of building current and specialized competencies than degree curricula alone can provide. IT micro-credentials may therefore offer a practical way to develop focused capabilities in areas such as cybersecurity, cloud computing, data analytics, programming, artificial intelligence applications, and digital project work. Yet the existence of such credentials does not guarantee that students will see them as worthwhile. Before pursuing a credential, students may question whether it is recognized, whether it can contribute to future study pathways, and whether it signals real labor-market value.\par
\par
Thailand provides a highly relevant setting for studying this issue. OECD (2025) highlights the importance of improving lifelong learning systems, strengthening skills development, and creating more flexible pathways for learning in Thailand. The report also notes the role of national initiatives such as Thai MOOC and the National Credit Bank System in supporting broader participation in learning and the recognition of prior learning and skills. These developments suggest that Thailand is actively building an ecosystem in which shorter and more flexible forms of certification may become more important. However, policy development does not automatically determine student perception. It remains necessary to understand how learners themselves evaluate emerging credential forms.\par
\par
At the same time, existing literature suggests that micro-credentials face important credibility challenges. Varadarajan et al. (2023) show that different stakeholders expect different things from micro-credentials, with institutions and employers placing considerable weight on trust, clarity, and evidence of competencies. Raj et al. (2024) identify barriers such as unclear definitions, weak accreditation, and insufficient quality assurance. Ha et al. (2024) similarly show that employers may recognize possible benefits in micro-credentials while still expressing doubts about their value. These findings indicate that micro-credentials are evaluated not only on the basis of learning content, but also on the basis of visible signals that suggest recognition, quality, and external relevance.\par
\par
For students, these signals may be especially important because the future value of a micro-credential is not directly observable at the time of decision-making. Students often must decide whether a credential is worth pursuing before they can know its academic or labor-market payoff. As a result, they may rely on features such as formal recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement as cues that help them judge whether the credential itself is credible, reliable, and worthwhile. This issue is central to the present study.\par
\par
{\b 1.2 Statement of the Problem}\par
Micro-credentials are increasingly promoted as flexible and employment-relevant forms of learning, and Thailand is developing policy frameworks that support shorter and more modular educational pathways. However, the expansion of micro-credential offerings does not ensure that students will perceive those offerings as credible or valuable. A central problem remains: students may not know whether a micro-credential is formally recognized, whether it can contribute to future qualifications, or whether it carries meaningful industry relevance.\par
\par
This problem is particularly important for Thai IT students. Because IT fields evolve quickly, students may have strong motivation to pursue targeted short-form learning. Yet they also face uncertainty in deciding which credentials are worth their effort. If students do not trust the meaning or usefulness of a credential, they may be less likely to value it, even if policymakers and providers regard it as beneficial. In this sense, the challenge is not merely one of access or availability. It is also one of credibility and perceived value.\par
\par
Previous research has provided important insights into implementation, employability, and learner engagement in micro-credentials. However, much of the student-side quantitative literature remains focused on adoption, intention, or continuance rather than on the credibility of the credential itself. As a result, there is still limited evidence on how concrete credential features shape credential trust and perceived value, particularly in the Thai context. Without such evidence, universities, digital learning providers, and policymakers may continue developing micro-credentials without knowing which features most strongly influence learner judgment.\par
\par
{\b 1.3 Research Objectives}\par
The general objective of this study is to examine how visible features of IT micro-credentials influence credential trust and perceived value among Thai IT students.\par
\par
The specific objectives are as follows:\par
1. To examine the effect of formal recognition on credential trust in IT micro-credentials among Thai IT students.\par
2. To examine the effect of stackability on credential trust in IT micro-credentials among Thai IT students.\par
3. To examine the effect of industry endorsement on credential trust in IT micro-credentials among Thai IT students.\par
4. To examine the effect of credential trust on the perceived value of IT micro-credentials among Thai IT students.\par
5. To examine the direct effects of formal recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement on the perceived value of IT micro-credentials among Thai IT students.\par
6. To examine whether credential trust mediates the relationship between these credential signals and perceived value.\par
\par
{\b 1.4 Research Questions}\par
This study is guided by the following research questions:\par
1. How does formal recognition affect credential trust in IT micro-credentials among Thai IT students?\par
2. How does stackability affect credential trust in IT micro-credentials among Thai IT students?\par
3. How does industry endorsement affect credential trust in IT micro-credentials among Thai IT students?\par
4. How does credential trust affect the perceived value of IT micro-credentials among Thai IT students?\par
5. Do formal recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement directly influence the perceived value of IT micro-credentials?\par
6. Does credential trust mediate the relationship between these credential signals and perceived value?\par
\par
{\b 1.5 Significance of the Study}\par
\par
{\b 1.5.1 Theoretical Significance}\par
This study contributes to the emerging literature on micro-credentials by moving beyond a simple adoption framework and focusing instead on how learners infer value from visible credential features. The study draws on signaling theory to explain why students may rely on recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement when evaluating a credential under uncertainty. It also draws on legitimacy logic to explain why these signals may influence credential trust. In doing so, the study offers a more focused explanation of how perceived value is formed at the student level in the context of micro-credentials.\par
\par
{\b 1.5.2 Practical Significance}\par
The findings may help universities, online learning providers, and designers of IT micro-credentials understand which features make a credential appear more credible and worthwhile to students. If recognition, stackability, or industry endorsement are found to increase credential trust and perceived value, these features can be emphasized in future micro-credential design. This would support the development of credentials that are not only available, but also meaningful to learners.\par
\par
{\b 1.5.3 Policy Significance}\par
At the policy level, the study is relevant to current efforts in Thailand to strengthen lifelong learning, digital skills development, and flexible educational pathways. As Thailand continues to develop systems such as Thai MOOC and credit-bank structures, evidence on how students judge new forms of certification can support more effective policy communication and better design of recognition systems. Understanding student perception is particularly important because policy success depends not only on institutional supply, but also on learner confidence in what is being offered.\par
\par
{\b 1.6 Scope of the Study}\par
This study focuses on IT micro-credentials in Thailand. The target population consists of undergraduate and postgraduate students enrolled in IT-related programs, including information technology, computer science, software engineering, information systems, and related digital disciplines at Thai universities.\par
\par
The study examines three main independent variables represented as visible credential signals: formal recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement. Credential trust is treated as the mediating variable, and perceived value of IT micro-credentials is treated as the primary dependent variable. Enrollment intention may be considered as a secondary exploratory outcome, but it is not the main focus of the study.\par
\par
Methodologically, the study is designed as a quantitative survey-experimental study using randomized vignette scenarios. Respondents will evaluate hypothetical IT micro-credential descriptions, and the analysis will focus on how differences in credential features affect trust and perceived value.\par
\par
{\b 1.7 Limitations of the Study}\par
This study has several limitations. First, it focuses on student perceptions rather than employer hiring behavior or actual labor-market outcomes. Therefore, the findings will explain how students judge micro-credentials rather than whether those judgments perfectly match employer responses. Second, because the study uses survey-based evaluations of hypothetical scenarios, it measures stated perceptions rather than real enrollment behavior. Third, the sampling strategy is likely to rely on purposive and convenience recruitment across Thai universities, which may limit full representativeness. Finally, because the study is situated in Thailand, its findings may not be directly generalizable to contexts with different higher education systems and recognition structures.\par
\par
Despite these limitations, the study remains valuable because student perception is itself an important part of whether micro-credentials gain traction. If students do not trust the credential, the intended policy and educational benefits may not be realized.\par
\par
{\b 1.8 Definition of Key Terms}\par
\par
{\b Micro-credentials}\par
Micro-credentials are short-form certifications that verify specific learning outcomes, knowledge, or skills achieved through a focused learning experience.\par
\par
{\b IT micro-credentials}\par
IT micro-credentials are micro-credentials related to information technology and digital fields, such as programming, cybersecurity, cloud systems, data analytics, digital tools, and related technical competencies.\par
\par
{\b Formal Recognition}\par
Formal recognition refers to the extent to which a micro-credential is officially acknowledged by universities, academic systems, or recognized higher-education credit structures.\par
\par
{\b Stackability}\par
Stackability refers to the extent to which a micro-credential can count toward future formal academic credit within a recognized higher-education pathway rather than remaining a standalone certificate.\par
\par
{\b Industry Endorsement}\par
Industry endorsement refers to the extent to which a micro-credential is co-developed and endorsed by a recognized IT industry association, employer consortium, or related professional body.\par
\par
{\b Student Trust (Credential Trust)}\par
Student trust in this study refers specifically to credential trust, meaning students' confidence that an IT micro-credential itself is credible, reliable, institutionally meaningful, and worthy of confidence. It does not refer to general trust in a provider or in higher education as a whole.\par
\par
{\b Perceived Value}\par
Perceived value refers to students' overall judgment that an IT micro-credential offers meaningful academic, professional, or personal benefit relative to the effort and sacrifice required to obtain it.\par
\par
{\b 1.9 Organization of the Thesis}\par
This thesis is organized into six chapters. Chapter 1 introduces the study, presents the research problem, objectives, research questions, significance, scope, limitations, and key terms. Chapter 2 reviews the literature relevant to micro-credentials, signaling theory, legitimacy, trust, and perceived value, and identifies the research gap addressed by the study. Chapter 3 presents the conceptual framework, hypotheses, and operationalization of variables. Chapter 4 explains the research methodology, including research design, population, sampling, instrument development, data collection, and data analysis procedures. Chapter 5 presents the research findings. Chapter 6 discusses the findings, outlines theoretical and practical implications, identifies limitations, and offers recommendations for future research.\par
